exercises.solution:
```python
def merge(left, right):
    out, i, j = [], 0, 0
    while i < len(left) and j < len(right):
        if right[j] < left[i]:        # strictly smaller: only then take from the right
            out.append(right[j]); j += 1
        else:                         # ties go to the left half, which came first
            out.append(left[i]); i += 1
    out.extend(left[i:])
    out.extend(right[j:])
    return out

def merge_sort(a):
    if len(a) <= 1:
        return list(a)
    mid = len(a) // 2
    return merge(merge_sort(a[:mid]), merge_sort(a[mid:]))
```

Everything in the left half came before everything in the right half in the original list, so breaking ties in favour of the left preserves the original order of equal items; by induction on the recursion, the whole sort is stable. Writing `if left[i] < right[j]: take left, else take right` looks equivalent but sends ties to the right, and the sort becomes unstable. The running time is $\Theta(n \log n)$ and the extra memory $\Theta(n)$.
